5, -0.00402069091796875, 0.033935546875, -0.02142333984375, -0.0271148681640625, 0.02197265625, -0.006465911865234375, 0.005947113037109375, 0.005901336669921875, 0.023468017578125, -0.005260467529296875, 0.00698089599609375, -0.000732421875, 0.001918792724609375, 0.034027099609375, 0.034149169921875, 0.0160064697265625, -0.024810791015625, 0.000736236572265625, -0.00733184814453125, 0.010345458984375, -0.0185394287109375, 0.008392333984375, 0.002410888671875, -0.0196685791015625, 0.009124755859375, 0.0172576904296875, -0.016357421875, -0.0030841827392578125, 0.0234375, 0.04150390625, 0.0037288665771484375, -0.004604339599609375, -0.00713348388671875, 0.0018320083618164062, 0.0166473388671875, 0.007305145263671875, -0.0208282470703125, -0.0033206939697265625, 0.0005731582641601562, -0.037750244140625, -0.0139007568359375, -0.00856781005859375, -0.0167083740234375, 0.0242767333984375, -0.0377197265625, -0.0007271766662597656, -0.022613525390625, -0.0245361328125, 0.0035076141357421875, -0.018035888671875, 0.01456451416015625, -0.0033016204833984375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents incrementally expand a textual memory bank as they learn from sequences of tasks. Here, a *self-improving agent* is defined as a learning system that autonomously augments its performance over time by accumulating experiences and adapting its memory or parameters based on past tasks, without resetting between tasks. This approach has achieved impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work frequently overlooks critical aspects relating to *reliability*\u2014specifically, issues of *fragility* (performance sensitivity across random seeds or task orders) and *underspecification* (ambiguous or insufficiently defined objectives)\u2014which raise concerns for reproducibility and real-world deployment. For instance, a navigation agent that succeeds 90% of the time on one random seed but only 50% on another exposes fragility, potentially causing critical failures in autonomous vehicles or service robots when deployed in the field. Similarly, an agent told \u201copen the door\u201d without clear indication of which door (if several exist) faces underspecification; in a hospital setting, this could risk accessing unauthorized, even dangerous, areas. Such failures undermine trustworthiness in safety-critical domains such as assistive robotics, healthcare, or automation.\n\n**Definitions: Fragility vs. Underspecification**  \nWe formally define and contrast these core concepts as follows (see Table 1 for summary):\n\n| Aspect             | Fragility                                | Underspecification                   |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Definition         | Sensitivity of agent performance to small perturbations in random seed, task order, or environment initialization. | Existence of multiple plausible strategies or agent behaviors due to ambiguous or incomplete task/environment specifications. |\n| Manifestation      | Large variance in reward/success across repeated runs; performance drops under order/task perturbation. | Multiple valid but unintended solutions; ambiguous or incomplete memory traces. |\n| Example            | Success drops from 90% (seed A) to 50% (seed B) on identical tasks. | Agent solves \u201copen the door\u201d without clarifying which door.            |\n\nWhile these concepts are distinct, edge cases and interactions can arise. Fragility relates to *variability*: small changes yield disproportionately large fluctuations in behavior or outcomes. Underspecification hinges on *ambiguity*: ill-defined goals permit several correct\u2014yet possibly undesirable\u2014responses. However, the two can overlap. For instance, ambiguous task instructions (underspecification) may increase sensitivity to stochastic learning or environment factors (fragility), leading to greater performance variation. In practice, both phenomena erode trust in real deployments: one by causing unreproducible results, the other by enabling unintended or unsafe behaviors.\n\nIn this context, *random seed* refers to the stochastic initialization used for agent training; different seeds yield different learning trajectories. *Task order* describes the sequence in which tasks are presented during training\u2014\u201cfixed order\u201d means tasks follow a standard curriculum, while \u201cshuffled order\u201d means tasks are randomly permuted per run. *Success* is the percentage of evaluation episodes in which the agent completes the task as specified by the environment. \u201cReward,\u201d in contrast, refers to a scalar score assigned by the environment for each agent action and completed subgoal.\n\nAn illustrative BabyAI case: fragility emerges if performance crashes when tasks are shuffled\u2014not only in simulation, but in real deployments, such shifting could mean a household robot's effectiveness plummets in a new apartment layout. Underspecification arises when an agent logs \u201cpicked up object\u201d without specifying which object, risking misaligned outcomes\u2014such as delivering the wrong medication to a patient. In such deployment scenarios, fragility can produce unpredictable failures across environments or runs, while underspecification can yield systematically unsafe actions due to ambiguous outcomes.\n\n**Experimental Scope and Method Selection**  \nWe systematically evaluate two representative paradigms of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) *Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA)* [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) *Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR)* [Lee & Chen, 2023]. Method selection was based on: (a) *broad adoption* (both EMA and RMR are among the top five most-cited memory agents on MiniGrid and BabyAI in the past three years, each cited in \u22658 peer-reviewed RL papers according to the RL Survey v2023.2), (b) methodological diversity\u2014EMA pursues explicit episodic memory growth, RMR adopts retrospective retrieval and pruning\u2014and (c) *state-of-the-art* status, operationalized as having produced top-3 leaderboard scores on BabyAI\u2019s public benchmark as of January 2024. *Direct compatibility* was further required: both methods provide official implementations with unified textual-memory interfaces, enabling uniform evaluation protocols in MiniGrid and BabyAI.\n\nAlternative methods were excluded based on at least one of the following, with explicit numbers where possible: (i) low recent adoption (e.g., agents X and Y accounted for <7% of MiniGrid leaderboard submissions per RL Survey v2023.2); (ii) architectural redundancy, defined as sharing >90% of memory module components with EMA or RMR (see Table 4), which yielded negligible observed differences in pilot runs; or (iii) requiring incompatible dependencies or memory formats (e.g., graph-structured or continuous representations requiring custom environment patches, as documented in [Zhou et al., 2023]). Resource limitations precluded adapting incompatible methods for this study. While EMA and RMR together cover approximately 65% of published memory-centric RL benchmarks on MiniGrid/BabyAI (see RL Survey v2023.2, Table A9, in main text), we acknowledge omitting agents with continuous, hardware-coupled, or hybrid memory structures. This limits immediate generalizability beyond textual-episodic memory agents in these environments. Benchmarking future work should aim for wider codebase harmonization and clearer reporting to enable broader synthesis.\n\n**Experimental Design and Statistical Rigor**  \nAgents were evaluated in MiniGrid and BabyAI, requiring multi-step navigation and interaction skills. Each agent underwent two regimes: (a) *Fixed task order* (canonical curriculum, tasks always presented in the same sequence), and (b) *Randomized task order* (\u201cshuffled order,\u201d distinct permutation per run). Environment and hyperparameter settings followed original baseline papers (details in Appendix A). For each configuration, we conducted five independent runs with distinct random seeds (N=5). We proceeded with N=5 to ensure parity with prior literature, which historically reports N=3 to N=5 due to similar resource constraints; this allowed baseline-aligned comparisons and enabled at least preliminary identification of major qualitative failure modes. Nevertheless, this small sample size imposes key limitations:\n\n- **Low statistical power:** N=5 is underpowered to reliably detect moderate effect sizes; statistical conclusions may flip as sample size increases. Power analysis suggests at least N=10\u201315 is needed for robust inference (see Appendix E for details).\n- **Sensitivity to outliers:** A single anomalous run can substantially affect mean, standard deviation (SD), and p-values.\n- **Unstable confidence intervals:** 95% confidence intervals (CIs) may be artificially narrow or wide.\n- **Type I/II error risk:** Increased likelihood of false positives or false negatives.\n- **Cautious interpretation:** Results should not be overgeneralized; they principally signal trends and prominent failure modes rather than enabling claims of precise effect sizes.\n\nTo partially mitigate these limitations:\n- We report SD and 95% CIs, indicating the spread and uncertainty of the results.\n- Bootstrap resampling (1,000 draws; see Appendix D) was used to estimate if mean differences persist under sampling variability.\n- Reported p-values arise from two-sample t-tests (unpaired, two-sided), but no corrections were applied for multiple comparisons; given low N and risk of inflated Type I error, all significance results are regarded as suggestive, not definitive, and effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d) are included to contextualize differences.\n- We explicitly recommend future experiments with larger N (\u226510\u201315) to validate these findings, as stronger conclusions must await wider, more statistically powered studies.\n\n**Rigorous Annotation and Memory Inspection**  \nTo detect underspecification and failure cases, two expert annotators (PhD students with RL expertise) independently reviewed episode summaries and textual memory banks using a standardized, environment-agnostic rubric. The rubric was developed through iterative piloting: initial draft criteria (drawn from RL diagnostics and human interpretability literature) were trialed on 20 pilot episodes, after which items were refined for clarity and completeness via annotator feedback and trial scoring. The finalized rubric included:\n\n1. *Completeness*: All required subgoals/steps present (e.g., navigation followed by interaction; missing subgoals frequently led to real-world errors such as incomplete deliveries or missing safety checks)\n2. *Unambiguity*: One-to-one mapping of goals to memory traces (ambiguity often resulted in the agent misidentifying critical items\u2014such as confusing similar objects or locations)\n3. *Outcome Traceability*: Clear link between actions and outcomes (failure here created problems like not being able to audit or diagnose task execution)\n4. *No Contradiction*: No conflicting/ambiguous steps present (contradictions could cause the agent to attempt mutually exclusive or unsafe actions)\n\nRubric criteria were clarified with concrete examples during rubric development. For example, a borderline case involved an episode where the agent logged \u201cdelivered package\u201d without specifying which package; Annotator 1 flagged it as ambiguous, while Annotator 2 argued the context was sufficient. A third, blinded adjudicator resolved ambiguity in favor of marking as underspecified unless context uniquely identified the referent. We provide additional representative disagreement cases and resolution rationale in Appendix B. Annotators applied the rubric independently and blind to agent type. Inter-rater reliability was strong (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81). Disagreement cases (11"}}